  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how nanoreactors are designed for three broad uses: catalysis, energy storage, and biomedical applications. Across the panels, the central idea is that nanoscale compartments and shells can control where molecules go, how fast they react, and what products form.\n\nFor catalysis, the drawings emphasize selective transport and multistep chemistry. Microporous shells act like molecular sieves, allowing some reactants to enter while excluding others. Other circular architectures show hydrophobic and hydrophilic regions for molecular recognition, and multish­elled or interconnected chambers appear to support cascaded reactions by separating sequential steps while still permitting controlled exchange.\n\nFor energy storage, the nanoreactors seem to confine active sulfur species and guide ion movement. One schematic shows polysulfides retained inside a Si/SiO2@C-S hybrid structure, suggesting suppression of shuttle effects. Another illustrates Janus nanoreactors with directional ion diffusion channels, and a final sketch indicates that internal void space can buffer volume expansion, improving structural stability.\n\nIn biomedicine, the figure highlights diagnostic and therapeutic roles. Nanoreactor chips appear to assist laser desorption/ionization mass spectrometry for biomarker detection, while targeted drug delivery and bioorthogonal catalytic reactions are shown killing unhealthy cells and helping restore healthy cells.",
